{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_01a99710b442de920169bb0a2e9cc48191b33806de90e86bc6", "created": 1773865518, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "(\"entity\"<|>PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA<|>object<|>A cancer (lymphoma) that occurs in the central nervous system, primarily the brain and spinal cord, and sometimes involves the eyes; it typically does not spread far beyond the CNS/eyes and requires specialized diagnostic testing after diagnosis to assess extent and plan treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>VITREORETINAL LYMPHOMA<|>object<|>The term used when primary CNS lymphoma involves the eyes, particularly affecting structures such as the retina and/or vitreous; represents ocular involvement of CNS lymphoma.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BRAIN<|>place<|>A major organ of the central nervous system; primary CNS lymphoma commonly occurs here, and it is directly connected to the eyes through the optic nerves, explaining potential ocular spread.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>SPINAL CORD<|>place<|>A central nervous system structure connected with cerebrospinal fluid spaces; primary CNS lymphoma can occur here and may be evaluated when cancer cells are suspected in spinal fluid or when symptoms suggest spinal lesions.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYES<|>place<|>Sensory organs connected directly to the brain via the optic nerves; can be involved by primary CNS lymphoma, prompting thorough ophthalmic evaluation.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>OPTIC NERVES<|>place<|>Nerve pathways connecting the retina/eyes to the brain, transmitting visual signals; provide a direct connection that helps explain why CNS lymphoma can involve the eyes.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>RETINA<|>place<|>The back layer of the eye where lymphoma often forms in ocular involvement; converts visual input into signals sent through the optic nerves to the brain.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>VITREOUS<|>place<|>The clear gel filling the eye; lymphoma cells can invade it, and it can be sampled via vitrectomy/biopsy to detect cancer cells.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PUPILS<|>place<|>Openings in the iris that are dilated using eye drops during an eye exam to improve visualization of internal eye structures.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>CEREBROSPINAL FLUID (CSF)<|>object<|>A protective/nourishing fluid that flows in and around hollow areas in the brain and spinal cord; can be removed and tested for cancer cells to help confirm primary CNS lymphoma and guide treatment planning.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>SPINAL FLUID<|>object<|>Cerebrospinal fluid in the spinal canal; testing may reveal cancer cells that indicate involvement/spread to CNS compartments and may or may not cause symptoms.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>SPINAL COLUMN<|>place<|>The bony structure housing the spinal cord and spinal fluid spaces; accessed in lumbar puncture using a long thin needle in the lower back.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LOWER BACK<|>place<|>The anatomical area numbed for lumbar puncture so a needle can be inserted to remove cerebrospinal fluid for testing.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>CHEST<|>place<|>Body region sometimes imaged (often with CT) after diagnosis to check for cancer outside the CNS/eyes, though such spread is uncommon.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>ABDOMEN<|>place<|>Body region imaged (often with CT) to look for cancer spread outside the CNS/eyes as part of staging/extent evaluation.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PELVIS<|>place<|>Body region imaged (often with CT) to assess for cancer spread outside the CNS/eyes.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BONE MARROW<|>place<|>The spongy center of many bones; may be evaluated (e.g., with biopsy) when assessing extent of disease.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>TESTICLES<|>place<|>An anatomical site that may be evaluated (for example by ultrasound) as part of post-diagnosis assessment in some cases.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYE DOCTOR<|>character<|>A clinician who performs a thorough eye exam for people diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma, using tools such as a slit lamp and dilation drops to examine internal eye structures.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>OPHTHALMOLOGIST<|>character<|>A medical eye specialist referenced as performing close-up eye examination with a slit lamp to assess for ocular involvement by CNS lymphoma.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LAB<|>place<|>A testing facility where collected samples such as cerebrospinal fluid or vitreous are sent to be analyzed for cancer cells and other clinically relevant findings.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYE EXAM<|>activity<|>A thorough ophthalmic evaluation recommended for everyone diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma; includes examining the retina, optic nerve, and other eye structures, often using dilation drops and a slit lamp.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYE EXAMINATION<|>activity<|>A referenced clinical assessment of the eyes (same concept as eye exam) used to detect ocular involvement of primary CNS lymphoma and guide diagnosis.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>SLIT LAMP<|>object<|>A specialized microscope with a bright light used in an eye doctor’s office to examine the retina, optic nerve, and other parts of the eye in detail.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>MICROSCOPE<|>object<|>An optical instrument referenced as part of the slit lamp device, enabling magnified visualization of eye structures.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BRIGHT LIGHT<|>object<|>The illumination component of a slit lamp used to provide detailed visualization of internal eye structures during examination.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYE DROPS<|>object<|>Drops administered to widen (dilate) the pupils before slit-lamp examination, improving the clinician’s view inside the eye.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>DILATION (PUPIL DILATION)<|>activity<|>The process of widening the pupils using eye drops to facilitate better visualization of the inside of the eye during an eye exam.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BIOPSY<|>activity<|>A diagnostic procedure to remove a small sample of tissue or fluid for testing; in this text it includes vitreous biopsy (via vitrectomy) and is contrasted with brain biopsy in confirming primary CNS lymphoma.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>VITREOUS BIOPSY<|>activity<|>Sampling of the vitreous (often via vitrectomy) when the vitreous looks unusual or visual symptoms are present, to test for lymphoma/cancer cells.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>VITRECTOMY<|>activity<|>A procedure in which the eye is numbed and a thin needle is inserted to remove a small amount of vitreous for testing; in some cases, vitrectomy alone can confirm primary CNS lymphoma without brain biopsy.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>ANESTHETIC<|>object<|>A numbing medication used before vitrectomy to reduce sensation in the eye during needle insertion and sample removal.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>THIN NEEDLE<|>object<|>A slender needle inserted into the eye during vitrectomy to remove a small vitreous sample for laboratory testing.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LUMBAR PUNCTURE<|>activity<|>A procedure (also called a spinal tap) that numbs the lower back and uses a long, thin needle to remove cerebrospinal fluid for testing; used to help confirm primary CNS lymphoma, assess spread to the spinal cord, and support treatment planning.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>SPINAL TAP<|>activity<|>Alternate name for lumbar puncture; performed to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing and evaluation of cancer involvement.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LONG, THIN NEEDLE<|>object<|>The needle used in lumbar puncture to access the lower spinal column and withdraw cerebrospinal fluid for laboratory testing.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>MRI<|>object<|>Magnetic resonance imaging; an imaging test that may be repeated to evaluate for lesions in the spine when symptoms exist or cancer cells are found in spinal fluid.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LESIONS IN THE SPINE<|>object<|>Abnormal areas that may be searched for by MRI when primary CNS lymphoma involvement of the spine is suspected.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>IMAGING<|>activity<|>Post-diagnosis evaluation to look beyond the CNS/eyes for disease; most commonly includes PET and/or CT and may include ultrasound of the testicles.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)<|>object<|>An imaging method that uses an injected radioactive drug to highlight cancer cells as bright spots on scans; used to evaluate the body for cancer after diagnosis.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (CT)<|>object<|>An imaging method that takes many x-rays from different angles and uses a computer to combine them into detailed images; CT of chest/abdomen/pelvis is recommended to look for spread.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>X-RAYS<|>object<|>Radiation-based images acquired in multiple angles during a CT scan, later combined by computer into a detailed composite image.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>COMPUTER<|>object<|>A system used in CT scanning to combine multiple x-ray images into one detailed image of a body part.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>CONTRAST AGENT<|>object<|>A substance injected into the bloodstream before CT to make areas of cancer easier to see on imaging.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PET/CT SCAN<|>event<|>A combined imaging study (PET plus CT) that can be performed with one or two machines depending on the cancer center; takes whole-body images to find cancer.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>RADIOACTIVE DRUG<|>object<|>A small-dose injected tracer used in PET imaging that causes cancer cells to appear as bright spots on PET scans.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>VEIN<|>place<|>A blood vessel into which the radioactive drug is injected prior to PET imaging.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>WHOLE-BODY IMAGES<|>object<|>Comprehensive imaging outputs (notably from PET/CT) used to look for cancer throughout the body.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>ULTRASOUND OF THE TESTICLES<|>activity<|>An imaging test that may be performed after diagnosis to evaluate the testicles; may be unnecessary if a whole-body PET/CT finds nothing.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BLOOD TESTS<|>activity<|>Laboratory tests performed on blood to establish extent of cancer and provide information needed for treatment planning; includes CBC, CMP, LDH, HIV testing, and possibly hepatitis B testing.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>COMPLETE BLOOD COUNT (CBC)<|>activity<|>A common blood test measuring red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets in a blood sample; used as part of evaluation after diagnosis.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>COMPREHENSIVE METABOLIC PANEL (CMP)<|>activity<|>A group of 14 blood tests providing information about blood sugar, calcium, electrolytes, and liver and kidney health; used for assessment and treatment planning.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LACTATE DEHYDROGENASE (LDH) TEST<|>activity<|>A blood test measuring the level of LDH enzyme; elevated LDH can suggest cell damage caused by cancer or other health problems.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LACTATE DEHYDROGENASE (LDH)<|>object<|>An enzyme involved in converting food into energy; measured in blood because high levels can indicate cell damage, including from cancer.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>HUMAN IMMUNODEFICIENCY VIRUS (HIV) BLOOD TEST<|>activity<|>A required blood test for everyone with primary CNS lymphoma to determine HIV status, because HIV-associated CNS lymphoma requires specialized management.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>HIV<|>object<|>A virus that can be related to primary CNS lymphoma; when present, it necessitates specialized care and antiretroviral therapy in addition to cancer treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>ANTIRETROVIRAL THERAPY<|>activity<|>A treatment used when HIV is present; described as needed in addition to cancer treatment for HIV-related primary CNS lymphoma.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>HEPATITIS B TESTING<|>activity<|>Testing for hepatitis B considered necessary if immunotherapy is planned and there is prior infection or risk, because immunotherapy can reactivate the virus and interfere with treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>HEPATITIS B VIRUS<|>object<|>A virus that can be reawakened (reactivated) by immunotherapy, potentially causing illness that interferes with cancer treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>IMMUNOTHERAPY<|>activity<|>A cancer treatment approach that can “reawaken” hepatitis B virus in previously infected or at-risk people; may require hepatitis B testing prior to use.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>DIAGNOSIS<|>event<|>The point at which primary CNS lymphoma is identified; triggers follow-up testing (eye exam, CSF testing, imaging, blood tests) to assess extent and plan treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BRAIN BIOPSY<|>activity<|>A diagnostic tissue sampling procedure from the brain; mentioned as sometimes avoidable when vitrectomy alone confirms primary CNS lymphoma.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BONE MARROW BIOPSY<|>activity<|>A procedure to sample bone marrow for testing; referenced as part of assessing disease extent (text introduces the topic of marrow and biopsy).)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA<|>BRAIN<|>Primary CNS lymphoma commonly occurs in the brain as part of the central nervous system involvement described in the text.<|>10)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA<|>SPINAL CORD<|>Primary CNS lymphoma can occur in the spinal cord, and evaluation may be needed if CSF shows cancer cells or symptoms suggest spinal involvement.<|>8)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA<|>EYES<|>Primary CNS lymphoma can involve the eyes, prompting recommended thorough eye evaluation for all diagnosed patients.<|>9)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>EYES<|>OPTIC NERVES<|>The eyes are connected directly to the brain by the optic nerves, transmitting visual information and forming a route relevant to ocular involvement discussion.<|>9)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>RETINA<|>OPTIC NERVES<|>The retina converts vision into signals that are sent through the optic nerves to the brain.<|>9)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>RETINA<|>EYES<|>The retina is located in the back of the eye and is a common site of ocular lymphoma involvement described.<|>9)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>VITREOUS<|>EYES<|>The vitreous is the clear gel that fills the eye and can be invaded by lymphoma cells.<|>9)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>VITREORETINAL LYMPHOMA<|>PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA<|>Vitreoretinal lymphoma is the term used when primary CNS lymphoma involves the eyes (retina/vitreous).<|>10)##\n(\"relationship\"<|>VITREORETINAL LYMPHOMA<|>VITREOUS<|>The condition includes invasion/involvement of the vitreous, whi